
Do you know how multiple transactions can update the same database record without compromising data consistency?

Understanding Optimistic Locking and Pessimistic Locking is essential for Java developers working with Spring Boot, JPA, Hibernate, and Microservices.

Optimistic Lock vs Pessimistic Lock

Two different approaches to handle concurrent access to data

Optimistic Locking

Assumes low conflict between transactions

What is Optimistic Locking?
Optimistic locking assumes that multiple transactions can read and update data without locking. It checks for conflicts at the time of update using a version or timestamp.

Product Table

ID	NAME	PRICE	VERSION
1	Laptop	50000	1

Diagram: Read Data (version = 1) → Update Data (if version = 1) → Update Successful (version = 2), or Version Mismatch (OptimisticLockException).

How It Works?

Read the data along with a version number.
Make changes to the data.
At update time, check if the version is still the same.
If the version matches, update the data and increment the version. If not, throw an exception.

Example (JPA - Optimistic Locking)

java
@Entity
public class Product {
    @Id
    private Long id;

    @Version    // Used for optimistic locking
    private Long version;

    // other fields
}

Use Cases

Read-heavy applications
Low chance of data conflicts
Web applications
Scenarios where conflict handling is acceptable (retry mechanism)
Pessimistic Locking

Assumes high conflict between transactions

What is Pessimistic Locking?
Pessimistic locking assumes that multiple transactions may try to modify the same data. It locks the data while it is being read, preventing other transactions from accessing it.

Product Table (Locked)

ID	NAME	PRICE	STATUS
1	Laptop	50000	Locked

Diagram: Transaction 1 Reads Data (LOCKED); Transaction 2 (Waits); Transaction 3 (Waits); then Update Data and Release Lock.

How It Works?

Acquire a database lock (e.g., SELECT ... FOR UPDATE).
Other transactions are blocked until the lock is released.
Perform the update.
Release the lock after the transaction is completed.

Example (JPA - Pessimistic Locking)

java
@Transactional
public Product getProductForUpdate(Long id) {
    return entityManager.find(
        Product.class,
        id,
        LockModeType.PESSIMISTIC_WRITE  // Locks for write
    );
}

Use Cases

Write-heavy applications
High chance of data conflicts
Banking and financial systems
Inventory management
When data consistency is critical
Key Differences
Aspect	Optimistic Locking	Pessimistic Locking
Approach	Assumes low conflict	Assumes high conflict
Lock	No lock while reading	Locks the data while reading
Conflict Handling	Uses version/timestamp	Blocks other transactions
Performance	Better (no locking overhead)	May reduce performance
Use Case	Read-heavy, low conflict	Write-heavy, high conflict
Example	@Version in JPA	LockModeType.PESSIMISTIC_WRITE
When to Use Which?

Use Optimistic Locking if:

Data conflicts are rare.
Your application is read-heavy.
You want better performance.
You can handle version conflicts (e.g., retry the transaction).

Use Pessimistic Locking if:

Data conflicts are frequent.
Data consistency is critical.
Your application is write-heavy.
You want to prevent other transactions from accessing it.
